latest_plan = "Based on our reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Identify the Primary Shape (Template):\n   a. Iterate through all non-black colors (1-9) in the input grid.\n   b. For each color, find all connected regions.\n   c. Select the largest connected region as the primary shape.\n   d. Store this shape as a set of coordinates with their corresponding color.\n\n2. Find Isolated Pixels:\n   a. Scan the entire grid.\n   b. Identify all non-black pixels that are not part of the primary shape.\n   c. Store these as a list of tuples (row, column, color).\n\n3. Generate Transformations of the Primary Shape:\n   a. Create functions for rotating and mirroring the shape.\n   b. Generate all possible rotations (0°, 90°, 180°, 270°).\n   c. Generate horizontal and vertical mirror images of each rotation.\n   d. Store all these transformations.\n\n4. Process Each Isolated Pixel:\n   For each isolated pixel:\n   a. Iterate through all transformations of the primary shape.\n   b. For each transformation:\n      i. Try placing the transformed shape at different positions around the isolated pixel.\n      ii. For each placement, calculate a score based on:\n          - Number of new colored squares added.\n          - Adherence to grid boundaries.\n          - Non-overlap with existing colored squares.\n   c. Select the transformation and placement with the highest score.\n   d. If a valid placement is found, apply it to a copy of the grid.\n\n5. Combine Results:\n   a. Start with a copy of the original input grid.\n   b. For each isolated pixel, apply the best transformation found in step 4.\n   c. Ensure that all original colored pixels are preserved.\n\n6. Validate the Solution:\n   a. Check that the final grid doesn't exceed the original dimensions.\n   b. Verify that all original colored pixels are in their original positions.\n   c. Ensure that no black pixels from the original grid have been colored.\n\n7. Return the Final Grid:\n   Convert the processed grid back into a ColoredGrid object and return it.\n\nImplementation Considerations:\n- Use efficient data structures (e.g., sets for quick lookups, dictionaries for storing shapes).\n- Implement helper functions for common operations like rotation, mirroring, and checking grid validity.\n- Use a scoring system that prioritizes maximizing new colored areas while respecting constraints.\n- Optimize the placement search to avoid unnecessary computations (e.g., skip placements that would obviously go out of bounds).\n\nThis plan should be able to handle all the test cases we've seen and should be generalizable to similar problems following the same pattern. The key is in correctly identifying the primary shape, generating all possible transformations, and then finding the optimal placement for each isolated pixel."
